WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.831
Ten o'clock on a busy Saturday at the corner shop.

2
00:00:05.831 --> 00:00:08.650
The Big Blue Vans booking service goes quiet.

3
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.094
Every call to it hangs for two seconds, and then fails.

4
00:00:12.094 --> 00:00:16.103
And each time, a customer at the counter stands there and waits.

5
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:23.531
A service, here, is another program the shop's program calls over the network, like the van company's booking line.

6
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:27.185
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:27.185 --> 00:00:29.822
The van company is the one that's broken.

8
00:00:29.822 --> 00:00:32.267
So why is my shop the one that's slow?

9
00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:34.778
Because the shop keeps dialing.

10
00:00:34.778 --> 00:00:37.675
The first customer waits two seconds, for a no.

11
00:00:37.675 --> 00:00:40.634
The next one waits two seconds, for the same no.

12
00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:44.456
Every call holds a thread, and the queue at the counter grows.

13
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:46.824
And the usual cure makes it worse.

14
00:00:46.824 --> 00:00:53.171
Retry three times, and each customer waits three times as long, for an answer that was never coming.

15
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.975
It's like phoning a shop that's closed for the day, and deciding the fix is to phone it faster.

16
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:06.844
What we'd like is for the shop to notice that the line is dead, stop dialing for a while, and now and then, check whether it's back.

17
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:12.346
The shop's back room already has a thing that does exactly that: the fuse box.

18
00:01:12.346 --> 00:01:19.449
When something goes wrong in the wiring, the circuit breaker trips and cuts the power, so the fault can't do more damage.

19
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:20.521
Let's map it.

20
00:01:20.521 --> 00:01:23.151
The wire is the call to the van company.

21
00:01:23.151 --> 00:01:26.438
Closed means the power flows, so calls go through.

22
00:01:26.438 --> 00:01:32.486
After a run of failures, it trips to open: calls fail at once, without even trying the vans.

23
00:01:32.486 --> 00:01:38.074
After a cool-down, it goes half-open, and lets one test call through, called a probe.

24
00:01:38.074 --> 00:01:40.047
If the probe works, it closes.

25
00:01:40.047 --> 00:01:41.559
If not, it opens again.

26
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.151
Yes, closed means the calls go through.

27
00:01:44.151 --> 00:01:48.429
Think of a closed loop of wire: the current can get all the way round.

28
00:01:48.429 --> 00:01:49.957
Open means there's a gap.

29
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:52.476
Let's use small numbers.

30
00:01:52.476 --> 00:01:58.041
The breaker opens after three failures in a row, and stays open for thirty seconds.

31
00:01:58.041 --> 00:01:59.985
A booking fails: closed, one.

32
00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:01.662
Another one: closed, two.

33
00:02:01.662 --> 00:02:04.008
A third: that's three, so it trips.

34
00:02:04.008 --> 00:02:06.288
Open, for the next thirty seconds.

35
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.366
Now the next booking arrives.

36
00:02:08.366 --> 00:02:12.999
The breaker is open, so it's turned away at once, with CallNotPermitted.

37
00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.766
No call to the van company, and no waiting.

38
00:02:15.766 --> 00:02:19.433
The customer hears no right away, and can try Pedal Post.

39
00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:21.962
Now the tricky moment.

40
00:02:21.962 --> 00:02:26.532
The breaker opened at 10:00:00, with a thirty second cool-down.

41
00:02:26.532 --> 00:02:30.231
At 10:00:31, eight threads call at the same moment.

42
00:02:30.231 --> 00:02:32.914
How many calls reach the van company?

43
00:02:32.914 --> 00:02:34.075
Pause and guess.

44
00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:39.910
Let's be fair to the obvious code first.

45
00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:45.225
Each thread reads the state, sees that the cool-down is over, and sets it to half-open.

46
00:02:45.225 --> 00:02:48.951
But two threads can both read open before either one sets it.

47
00:02:48.951 --> 00:02:53.899
Then several probes go out together, to a service that's barely back on its feet.

48
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.330
So the move from open to half-open is a compare-and-set.

49
00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.818
Read it as: update it only if nobody beat me to it.

50
00:03:01.818 --> 00:03:02.776
All eight try.

51
00:03:02.776 --> 00:03:05.306
Exactly one wins, and runs the probe.

52
00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:09.957
The other seven find the state already changed, and are turned away.

53
00:03:09.957 --> 00:03:11.393
So the answer is one.

54
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.990
Now look at what it's made of: three patterns from this track, in one class.

55
00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:21.433
Its states are sealed records with a transition, as in the State lesson.

56
00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:25.568
Its call wraps any operation and keeps its shape, like a decorator.

57
00:03:25.568 --> 00:03:30.567
And its state lives in an AtomicReference, a holder that threads can swap safely.

58
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:36.039
Here's the transition after a failed call, applied in one atomic step.

59
00:03:36.039 --> 00:03:37.985
A failed probe opens it again.

60
00:03:37.985 --> 00:03:41.227
A closed breaker that reaches the threshold trips.

61
00:03:41.227 --> 00:03:43.692
A closed one below it counts one more.

62
00:03:43.692 --> 00:03:45.443
And an open one stays open.

63
00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:47.999
Kotlin says the same thing with a when.

64
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.018
One more choice makes it testable.

65
00:03:51.018 --> 00:03:54.631
Time comes from a Clock passed in through the constructor.

66
00:03:54.631 --> 00:04:00.611
So a test hands it a fake clock, and moves time forward 31 seconds at once, instead of sleeping.

67
00:04:00.833 --> 00:04:05.576
A test that sleeps for thirty seconds is a test that nobody runs twice.

68
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.104
The fuse box picture breaks in two places.

69
00:04:09.104 --> 00:04:12.203
A real breaker stays off until a person resets it.

70
00:04:12.203 --> 00:04:15.923
The software one tries again by itself, after the cool-down.

71
00:04:15.923 --> 00:04:19.208
And a real breaker reacts to the current in the wire.

72
00:04:19.208 --> 00:04:25.221
The software one only counts failures, so you must tell it which errors mean the service is sick.

73
00:04:25.433 --> 00:04:30.195
A van company that answers, no vans free in your area, is working perfectly.

74
00:04:30.195 --> 00:04:34.769
That's a polite no, not a sick service, and it must not trip the breaker.

75
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:36.929
So here's the rule.

76
00:04:36.929 --> 00:04:45.335
Count only the failures that mean the service is sick, and let one probe, chosen by compare-and-set, decide when it has recovered.

77
00:04:45.567 --> 00:04:46.559
Two guardrails.

78
00:04:46.559 --> 00:04:53.700
Retry in one layer only, outside the breaker, and never retry a rejection: that's the breaker doing its job.

79
00:04:53.700 --> 00:05:03.421
And a breaker lives in one program's memory: two copies of the program have two breakers, so a limit for the whole shop needs something they share.

80
00:05:04.333 --> 00:05:06.005
So, why was my shop slow?

81
00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:07.610
Because it kept dialing.

82
00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:15.703
A breaker counts the sick failures, trips, turns callers away at once, and lets one probe find out when the line is back.

83
00:05:15.933 --> 00:05:16.706
Quick check.

84
00:05:16.706 --> 00:05:21.661
The breaker is closed, with two failures counted, and the threshold is three.

85
00:05:21.661 --> 00:05:23.142
The next call succeeds.

86
00:05:23.142 --> 00:05:24.493
What's the state now?

87
00:05:27.667 --> 00:05:29.546
Closed, with zero failures.

88
00:05:29.546 --> 00:05:31.426
A success resets the count.

89
00:05:31.426 --> 00:05:34.001
Only three failures in a row trip it.
